{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe lexical tier reaches semantic recall@5 of 0.823. ADR 0122 measured one candidate that beats it distinguishably at k=5: `vector` — a digest of each ticket, embedded and ranked by cosine similarity — at 0.876, McNemar p=0.0213, recovering 13 pairs and losing 3. That single comparison is this tier's whole justification, worth 93 otherwise-invisible duplicate pairs.\n\nrebar runs against diverse providers, so this tier must not be reachable through one vendor only, and an installation with no provider must still get similar-ticket surfacing.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Portability is delivered by the lexical tier, not by this one.** The index sibling ships retrieval and the surfacing sibling the advisory, with no provider, no credentials and no network between them. This tier is an opt-in ranking upgrade that requires *some* embedding provider and degrades to lexical whenever one is absent or failing.\n\n**Vendor-neutrality comes from a seam rebar's runtime already ships.** The installed distribution is `pydantic-ai-slim` 1.107.9, already the LLM runtime behind `src/rebar/llm/providers.py`. It exposes a first-class embeddings API — `infer_embedding_model`, `Embedder`, `EmbeddingModel` and `EmbeddingSettings`, each verified present on `pydantic_ai.embeddings`. Dispatching a `provider:model` string through it is the shape `providers.py` already uses for chat models.\n\n**A missing backend is an expected state, and AC2's two cases are already pinned.** `pydantic-ai-slim` is not in rebar's core `dependencies`; it arrives through `[agents]`, and the Bedrock default needs `[bedrock]`. In this dev install `pydantic_ai.embeddings`, `.bedrock`, `.openai` and `.test` import, while `.cohere`, `.google` and `.voyageai` raise `ImportError` naming their own optional group — an ordinary expected state that must degrade exactly like a provider failure. `pydantic-ai-slim[openai]` and `[bedrock]` are already declared in the `dev` extra (`pyproject.toml:315`, `:318`), put there so their tests run in CI rather than skip — so AC2 is pinned without this ticket touching any dependency list.\n\n**Both model choices are configuration, and both are unset by default.** The two keys are `[llm] embedding_model` and `[llm] similar_digest_model`, read from `REBAR_LLM_EMBEDDING_MODEL` and `REBAR_LLM_SIMILAR_DIGEST_MODEL`, declared on `LLMConfig` in `src/rebar/llm/config.py` beside the existing `model` key and resolved through the same provider-qualified-string convention. Neither ships an active default: this tier is opt-in, and with `[llm] embedding_model` unset the query is the lexical tier's. `bedrock:amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0` (1,024 dimensions, cosine) and `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0` are the values ADR 0122 measured and the values this ticket documents as recommended, not values rebar applies on a fresh install.\n\n**The edit set two new `[llm]` keys require.** `src/rebar/llm/config.py` declares the fields and reads them in `LLMConfig.from_env`. `docs/env-vars.md` is GENERATED by `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py` and is drift-tested (`tests/unit/test_generated_surface_markers.py`, `tests/unit/test_llm_config_pointer.py`), so it is regenerated and committed in the same change. `tests/golden/config_surface.json` and `tests/unit/test_config_surface_golden.py` are NOT touched: that golden inventories only the canonical keys derived from `rebar._config_schema._SECTIONS`, which carries no `[llm]` section — verified against `origin/main`, where the golden holds zero `llm.*` keys. The mechanism census records an `[llm]` key under its environment name, as the 40 existing `env_var::REBAR_LLM_*` baseline entries show and the zero `config_key::` entries naming `llm` confirm, so each new key carries a `# mechanism-ok: env_var REBAR_LLM_EMBEDDING_MODEL — 2041-75d6-2a2f-4be9` marker (and the matching one for the digest key) at its definition site.\n\n**Stored vectors are keyed by what produced them.** Because both model strings are operator-overridable and embedding spaces are not comparable across models, the index's validity key folds the embedding-model identity and the pinned digest-prompt commit in alongside the sibling's existing freshness key. Changing either config invalidates and re-embeds the affected entries rather than cosine-ranking across incompatible spaces.\n\n**Cosine ranking requires full current-key coverage; anything less is a whole-corpus lexical query.** A per-ticket embedding call can fail or time out partway through a build or a re-embed, leaving some live entries carrying a current-validity-key vector and some not — the natural outcome of the denial/timeout state below, and of an interrupted re-embed after a config change. rebar never ranks across a partially vectored corpus and never ranks across two embedding spaces: the query cosine-ranks only when EVERY live entry carries a vector under the current validity key, and otherwise returns the lexical tier's results for the whole corpus with a diagnostic, exiting 0 — the same disposition as a provider failure. This is a read-path rule, so an incomplete build or re-embed degrades the ranking and never corrupts it.\n\n**Digest step.** The 4-field prompt pinned at `f147e08242f`, input capped at 40,000 characters, per ADR 0122's architecture section. The digest is the weakest link: `vector` is *not* distinguishable from `vector-raw`, which skips it (p=0.0654, p=0.0625), and survives only because `vector-raw` also fails to beat `fts5` (p=0.6636) — a non-transitive chain ADR 0122 states plainly.\n\n**Two digest failure states, told apart by whether a digest object was recovered.** (a) *Did not answer the task*: the reply yields no digest mapping at all — nothing JSON- or YAML-decodable to an object carrying any of the 4 fields. 2 of 630 hit this and both succeeded on retry, so this state is retried once. (b) *Parsed to empty fields*: a mapping is recovered but every field is empty — the three reply shapes ADR 0122 names (a `ticket_digest` wrapper key, YAML instead of JSON, JSON behind a prose preamble), each indistinguishable from success without a counter. This state increments `empties` and falls back, with no retry.\n\n**Ranking swaps behind an interface that already works.** The index, its freshness map and read-phase catch-up come from the index sibling [rebar:47c8-97ac-eff3-4e88]; the create advisory across the four surfaces [rebar:80a5-cbb1-ef06-4ba5] from the surfacing sibling. This tier replaces only the scoring function and adds the digest-and-embed build step. k stays at 5: ADR 0122's decision is scoped there, and at k=10 `vector`'s edge is indistinguishable (p=0.3438).\n\n`TestEmbeddingModel` (`pydantic_ai.embeddings.test`) is a deterministic test double, not evidence of portability. Any ratcheted mechanism this tier adds — the two `REBAR_LLM_*` model variables named above are the expected ones — carries a `# mechanism-ok:` marker at its definition site naming this ticket.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An embedding model configured as a `provider:model` string is resolved through `infer_embedding_model`, and retrieval ranks by cosine similarity over the resulting vectors. Checked by: a test configuring `TestEmbeddingModel` and asserting the ordering follows cosine similarity.\n- [ ] Configuring a model string for at least two distinct providers resolves to each provider's embedding model. Checked by: a test parameterized over `bedrock:` and `openai:` model strings asserting `infer_embedding_model` returns the matching model class for each; both backends are pinned by the `dev` extra, so the test FAILS rather than skips when one is absent.\n- [ ] No vendor name appears in a conditional in the retrieval or digest code path. Checked by: a test asserting no provider literal from `_PROVIDER_PREFIXES` occurs in the added modules outside a default-value string or a docstring.\n- [ ] The digest model is resolved from a configured provider-qualified model string rather than a hard-coded vendor model. Checked by: a test configuring a non-Bedrock digest model string and asserting the resolved model matches it.\n- [ ] Changing the configured embedding model invalidates the affected stored vectors. Checked by: a test building an index, reconfiguring `[llm] embedding_model`, and asserting the affected entries are re-embedded rather than reused.\n- [ ] Changing the pinned digest-prompt commit invalidates the affected stored vectors. Checked by: a test building an index, repinning the digest-prompt commit, and asserting the affected entries are re-embedded rather than reused.\n- [ ] A query whose live entries do not all carry a current-validity-key vector returns the lexical tier's results for the whole corpus with a diagnostic, and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test whose embedding provider fails after N of M tickets, asserting the advisory equals the lexical tier's output, the diagnostic is emitted, and no entry is cosine-ranked.\n- [ ] An interrupted re-embed after an embedding-model change never ranks across two embedding spaces. Checked by: a test building an index, changing `[llm] embedding_model`, failing the re-embed after a subset, and asserting the query returns the lexical tier's results rather than mixing old-key and new-key vectors, with the create exiting 0.\n- [ ] With `[llm] embedding_model` unset, retrieval returns the lexical tier's results and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test asserting the advisory matches the lexical tier's output when the key is unset.\n- [ ] `[llm] embedding_model` and `[llm] similar_digest_model` are unset by default. Checked by: a test asserting both resolve to `None` on a `LLMConfig` built from an environment and config file that set neither.\n- [ ] When the configured backend's optional dependency is not installed, retrieval falls back to lexical-only with a diagnostic naming the missing group, and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test configuring a model string whose backend raises `ImportError`, asserting exit status, advisory contents and the diagnostic.\n- [ ] When the configured provider raises a denial or times out, retrieval falls back to lexical-only with a diagnostic and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test parameterized over both failures asserting exit status, advisory contents and the diagnostic.\n- [ ] When a digest mapping is recovered but every field is empty, retrieval falls back to lexical-only with a diagnostic and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test asserting exit status, advisory contents and the diagnostic.\n- [ ] An `empties` counter records every digest reply that recovers a mapping with only empty fields. Checked by: a test feeding each of the three reply shapes ADR 0122 records and asserting the counter equals 3.\n- [ ] A build completes despite digest replies that parse to empty fields. Checked by: the same test asserting the build reaches completion and indexes the remaining tickets.\n- [ ] A digest reply yielding no recoverable mapping is retried once. Checked by: a test whose model returns unparseable prose once then succeeds, asserting exactly one retry is counted.\n- [ ] A digest that succeeds on retry is recorded as a success, not a failure. Checked by: the same test asserting the ticket is indexed and the failure counter is 0.\n- [ ] rebar's declared dependencies are unchanged by this ticket. Checked by: `pyproject.toml`'s dependency and optional-dependency lists are untouched in the diff.\n- [ ] `docs/env-vars.md` is regenerated and committed with both new variables present. Checked by: running `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py` leaves no diff, and the drift tests in `tests/unit/test_generated_surface_markers.py` pass.\n- [ ] `tests/golden/config_surface.json` is unchanged by this ticket. Checked by: the file is untouched in the diff and `tests/unit/test_config_surface_golden.py` passes.\n- [ ] Every module added or modified under `src/rebar/` is at most 800 lines. Checked by: the module-size gate.\n- [ ] `check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0, with any new mechanism admitted by a `# mechanism-ok:` marker naming this ticket. Checked by: running it.\n- [ ] `check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exits 0. Checked by: running it."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793611510104195001,"uuid":"a93abf21-f6cd-4049-a086-4df7d9aecc7d"}